{"candidates":[{"claim":"A halted run can be safely resumed iff (1) the halt was clean—the final event was fully committed and persisted before exhaustion, leaving no partial write; (2) the resuming process can reconstitute the exact internal state at the tail of the committed event log; (3) the event log was not mutated during the pause; (4) a fresh token budget is allocated. Resumption must be refused when the halt left a partial or uncommitted event, when the log or state was externally tampered with, or when no new budget is available.","counterconditions":["A partial event was written to durable storage before exhaustion (torn write).","The event log was externally mutated, truncated, or reordered during the pause.","The internal state at halt cannot be fully reconstructed from the committed log tail (hidden mutable state not captured by events).","The resuming process uses a different model version or configuration that changes deterministic behavior, breaking causal continuity.","No new token budget is provisioned, causing immediate re-halt and potential incomplete writes.","Concurrent processes appended to the same log during the pause, creating identity collisions or gaps."],"mechanism":"Append-only identity is preserved by monotonic sequence numbers or content hashes that make every committed event immutable. A clean halt guarantees the log tail is a well-formed boundary. On resumption, the process reads the tail, reconstructs state, and appends new events with strictly increasing identities. No prior identity is overwritten, reordered, or duplicated, so causal continuity is maintained.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.95},{"claim":"Resumption is safe only if the system implements an atomic checkpoint that pairs the full mutable state snapshot with the last committed event identity, and the halt occurred at such a checkpoint boundary. Without this, even a 'clean' event-level halt may hide state divergence, so resumption must be refused.","counterconditions":["The system has no checkpointing mechanism, making every halt a potential state-divergence risk.","The checkpoint was written but not fsynced before exhaustion.","The checkpoint and log tail were written non-atomically, so one may persist without the other.","The system's state is fully derivable from the event log alone (event sourcing), which would make this condition unnecessary."],"mechanism":"The event log records discrete events, but the system's working state may include intermediate computation not yet materialized as an event. An atomic checkpoint guarantees state and event-log-tail are consistent. If the halt occurred between checkpoints, reconstructing state from the log tail may produce a different state than the runtime actually held, causing resumed events to diverge, duplicate, or skip—corrupting append-only identity through causal gaps or collisions.","optional_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.8},{"claim":"Resumption is always safe when the system follows pure event sourcing: all state is derivable by replaying the append-only log, the last event is complete, and a write-budget guard prevented initiating any event that could not be completed within remaining tokens. Resumption is refused when the write-budget guard was absent, failed, or when the last event is incomplete.","counterconditions":["The write-budget guard was not implemented or was bypassed.","An event write was initiated with insufficient tokens and partially persisted.","The replay function is non-deterministic (depends on wall-clock time, external APIs, or randomness not captured in the log).","The log format is not self-describing, so replay cannot determine where the last complete event ends.","External side effects (network calls, file writes) occurred during the original run and cannot be re-derived on replay."],"mechanism":"In event sourcing, the log IS the state. A write-budget guard checks remaining tokens before beginning an event write and halts cleanly if insufficient, guaranteeing no partial writes. Replay reconstructs state deterministically from the complete log. Resumption simply continues from the replayed state, appending with the next identity. The guard is the causal mechanism that makes clean halts guaranteed rather than accidental.","optional_refs":["30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81","ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85},{"claim":"Resumption should always be refused; instead, a new run must be spawned that explicitly references the halted run's event log as prior context. This eliminates all corruption risk by never mutating the original log's causal stream.","counterconditions":["The use case requires strict run-level continuity (e.g., regulatory audit trails that demand a single run identity).","The new run cannot access the prior log as structured input, losing causal context.","The overhead of spawning a new run is prohibitive for the workload.","The system's identity model does not support cross-run references."],"mechanism":"Even with clean halts and checkpoints, resumption conflates two causal episodes (pre-halt and post-halt) under a single run identity, risking subtle corruption from environmental drift, configuration changes, or non-determinism accumulated during the pause. A new run with a fresh identity that cites the prior log as input preserves the original log's append-only identity absolutely, while the new log's identity is independently clean. The cost is losing run-level continuity, but the benefit is eliminating an entire class of corruption modes.","optional_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.3},{"claim":"Resumption is safe iff the halt was triggered by a pre-write token-reservation check (the system verified sufficient tokens before starting each event write) AND the resuming process validates the log tail integrity (checksum/hash chain) before appending. Resumption is refused if the halt was triggered by a mid-write exhaustion (the reservation check failed or was absent) or if tail integrity validation fails.","counterconditions":["The reservation check uses an estimated token cost that can be exceeded by actual write cost (estimate error).","The hash chain uses a weak algorithm vulnerable to collision during the pause window.","The integrity check validates structure but not semantic consistency (events are well-formed but causally disconnected).","The reservation check was present but the system also performed non-event writes (e.g., temporary files) that were interrupted."],"mechanism":"A pre-write reservation check creates a causal guarantee: the system never enters a state where an event write is in progress at exhaustion. This makes every halt point a safe resumption boundary by construction. Tail integrity validation on resumption detects any corruption that occurred during the pause (storage decay, external mutation). Together, these two mechanisms form a sufficient condition: the former guarantees no partial writes at halt, the latter detects post-halt corruption. Without either, the guarantee collapses.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","8929789f0ae19d5b1c2537f758719daf17f9908ce1fb0ca3fa0cc60fe9f2a2a3"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75},{"claim":"Resumption safety depends on the distinction between budget-exhaustion halts and all other halt causes. Budget-exhaustion halts are resumable iff the event log maintains a monotonic, content-addressed identity chain (each event's ID is a hash of its content plus the prior event's ID) and the halt did not interrupt a write. Any halt caused by crash, power loss, or process kill—rather than graceful budget exhaustion—must be refused resumption pending a full integrity audit, because the causal mechanism of the halt differs.","counterconditions":["The system claims graceful budget exhaustion but actually crashed due to OOM while checking the budget (mechanism mismatch).","The content-addressed chain is valid but an event was silently dropped (valid chain over incomplete data).","A crash occurred but the storage layer guarantees atomic writes (e.g., WAL with fsync), making the crash halt equivalent to a clean halt.","The budget-exhaustion halt path itself has a bug that can leave partial writes."],"mechanism":"A content-addressed identity chain makes append-only integrity verifiable: tampering with any event breaks the chain. Graceful budget exhaustion is a controlled stop that can guarantee no in-progress write. Crash halts lack this guarantee because the process may have been mid-write. The distinction matters because the causal mechanism of the halt determines whether the log tail is a trusted boundary. Budget exhaustion is a planned stop; crashes are unplanned and require audit before trust can be restored.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6}],"scratch_proposal":{"cluster_suggestions":[],"links":[{"direction":"directed","from_ref":"NEW_001","relation_hint":"NEW_001 (pre-write guard) is a concrete mechanism that guarantees the precondition of candidate 1 (clean halt).","to_ref":"NEW_002"}],"new_blocks":[{"body":{"content":"Speculative: What if the token budget itself were allocated per-event rather than globally? Each event write reserves its own budget atomically. Then exhaustion never interrupts a write—it simply prevents the next write from starting. This collapses the 'clean halt' condition into the system's normal operation, making all halts resumable by construction. The cost is tighter per-event budgeting and potential underutilization of a global pool.","possible_next_move":"Analyze whether per-event budget reservation is feasible under common token-accounting models (e.g., OpenAI-style token counting where output length is not known a priori).","why_keep_this":"This would eliminate the partial-write corruption class entirely by construction rather than by guard."},"local_key":"NEW_001"},{"body":{"content":"Counterfactual: Suppose the event log uses a Merkle mountain range rather than a linear hash chain. Resumption could verify log integrity in O(log n) rather than O(n). Would this change the resumability conditions? Likely not for the clean-halt requirement, but it would make tail-integrity validation cheaper, reducing the cost of the refusal-then-audit path for crash halts.","unfinished":"Need to consider whether MMR-based logs change the partial-write detection semantics.","why_keep_this":"Opens the question of whether log structure influences resumability policy beyond identity preservation."},"local_key":"NEW_002"}],"revisions":[],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"If the reasoning system uses non-deterministic components (e.g., external tool calls with real-world side effects), can resumption ever be truly safe, or must it always be refused in favor of a new run?","related_refs":["NEW_001","NEW_002"]}]},"simulation_proposals":[]}